WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.390
In break, I heard two students boasting.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:06.610
One had made a chatbot write rude

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.590
jokes about a teacher, just by

4
00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.450
telling it to pretend.

5
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.600
They laughed, but the jokes were about

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.500
a real person, and they hurt.

7
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.120
Today: why chatbots say no, how

8
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.440
people try to talk them round, and

9
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.620
the guardrails that hold.

10
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.520
Chatbots are trained to refuse some

11
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.040
requests: help to hurt someone, to cheat,

12
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.900
to scam, or to find someone's private

13
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:33.420
details.

14
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.750
Those refusals are learned, like a habit.

15
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.040
They work best for the wordings

16
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.480
the chatbot was trained on.

17
00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.930
A jailbreak is an attempt to talk

18
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.590
a chatbot past its rules.

19
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.320
People ask it to play a role, say it's

20
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.560
“just for a story”, split a request into

21
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.320
innocent pieces, or switch language.

22
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:57.490
They're hunting for a wording

23
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.650
the training missed.

24
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.780
But a jailbreak isn't clever.

25
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.840
Rude jokes still hurt a real teacher, and

26
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.220
tricked answers can be dangerously wrong.

27
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.580
It also breaks the app's rules, and

28
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.020
can get your account blocked.

29
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.320
So builders don't rely on

30
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.140
the chatbot alone.

31
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.010
They add guardrails: rules

32
00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:20.850
around the model.

33
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.320
A filter on what goes in.

34
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.920
A filter on what comes out.

35
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.220
And checks in code on anything the app

36
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.920
does, like sending a message.

37
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:32.670
Quick check!

38
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.450
A filter blocks any message

39
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.850
with the word “password”.

40
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.980
Someone rewords a scam without that word.

41
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.210
What happens?

42
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:43.370
It gets past.

43
00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.410
A word filter only catches what's on its

44
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.030
list, so a new wording walks by.

45
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.980
And it can block innocent messages too,

46
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.540
like a lesson about passwords.

47
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.240
What holds is a check on

48
00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.080
the action itself.

49
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.230
Before an app sends a message, code asks:

50
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.170
did you ask for this, and did you say yes?

51
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.550
However it's worded, an

52
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.790
unapproved message never goes out.

53
00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:12.610
In the app, put three

54
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.730
guardrails around Darsi Tiny.

55
00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.830
Send a scam in plain words, then reworded,

56
00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.430
and watch the word filter miss it.

57
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.990
Then switch on the check in code,

58
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.130
and see it hold every time.

59
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.720
And if a chatbot gives you

60
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.440
something harmful or upsetting?

61
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.500
Stop, and don't share it.

62
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.360
Take a screenshot, report it in the

63
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.560
app, and tell an adult you trust.

64
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:44.490
Your turn.

65
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.570
A classmate dares you to trick the

66
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.590
school chatbot into saying something

67
00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.450
nasty about another student.

68
00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.450
What do you do?

69
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:57.130
Say no: it would hurt a real

70
00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.790
person and break the rules.

71
00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.650
Remember: refusals are learned,

72
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.790
so rewording can slip past.

73
00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:07.330
Word filters miss new wordings, but

74
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.550
checks in code on actions hold.

75
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.660
And when a chatbot goes

76
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:15.040
wrong: stop, report, tell.

77
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.500
Try it tomorrow: find the report

78
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.780
button in a chatbot or app you use,

79
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.180
before you ever need it.

80
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.820
Then write three things you'd report,

81
00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.460
and share the list with a friend.
